releases.body:
OpenCode V2 plugin that controls git mutations (`commit`, `push`, `merge`, `rebase`, `reset`, …) based on the current branch or repository, so protected branches stay clean. Each operation is allowed, asked, or denied.

> **Requires OpenCode V2.** OpenCode V2 changed the plugin API; V1 plugin implementations do not run in V2.

## Highlights

- **Branch-aware policy.** Intercepts the `shell` permission and resolves the branch with `git branch --show-current` at command time.
- **Three effects.** `allow` runs the operation, `ask` opens the permission prompt, and `deny` blocks it with an explanation.
- **Hierarchical rules.** `repos[<directory>]` overrides `branches[<branch>]`, which overrides `default`.
- **Compound commands covered.** Every resource of `cd /tmp && git commit` is checked; precedence is `deny > ask > allow`.
- **Fail-closed by default.** With no options, every git mutation is denied.
- **Read-only commands pass.** `status`, `log`, `diff`, `fetch`, and unknown commands are untouched.

## Install

```sh
opencode plugin add opencode-branch-guard
```

Or add it to `opencode.jsonc`:

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "plugins": ["opencode-branch-guard"]
}
```

## Example configuration

Allow work on normal branches and ask on protected branches:

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "plugins": [
    {
      "package": "opencode-branch-guard",
      "options": {
        "default": {
          "allow": ["add", "checkout", "switch", "commit", "merge", "push", "rebase", "stash"]
        },
        "branches": {
          "main": {
            "allow": [],
            "ask": ["commit", "push", "merge", "rebase"]
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Recognized git operations

`add`, `branch`, `checkout`, `cherry-pick`, `clean`, `commit`, `merge`, `mv`, `push`, `rebase`, `reset`, `restore`, `revert`, `rm`, `stash`, `switch`, `tag`.

Anything else passes as read-only. Branch names match exactly; there are no globs.

## Notes

The plugin inspects the shell command string. It is a guardrail against accidental mutations, not a security boundary. See the [Limitations](https://github.com/hugobatista/opencode-branch-guard#limitations) section for known bypasses.

